    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d510">

                <pb facs="0561" n="553" sameAs="frus:frus1939v02/pg553"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.512351 Double/438</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bullitt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 4294</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-05-04">May 4, 1939</date>.<lb/>[Received May 13.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the Embassy’s
                    telegram No. 877 of May 3, 1939, 6 p.m.,<anchor n="84" xml:id="d510-f1"/> and to
                    its despatch No. 4145 of April 7, 1939, and to transmit as enclosures hereto the
                    duly initialed English and French texts of a draft of a convention between the
                    United States and France for the avoidance of double taxation and for mutual
                    administrative assistance.<anchor n="85" xml:id="d510-f2"/></p>
                <pb facs="0562" n="554" xml:id="pg554"/>
                <p>The meetings between the American and French Delegations for the discussion of
                    the draft commenced, as the Department is already aware, on April 3 and
                    terminated on the evening of May 3 with the initialing of the French and English
                    texts with respect to which agreement had been reached by the French negotiators
                    and Messrs. King, Turkel, Fullerton,<anchor n="86" xml:id="d510-f3"/> and
                        Hulley<anchor n="87" xml:id="d510-f4"/> (the latter Embassy officers having
                    participated in the negotiations).</p>
                <p>As stated in its telegram of yesterday’s date,<anchor n="88" xml:id="d510-f5"/>
                    the Embassy regards the draft which is submitted as very satisfactory since it
                    establishes the basis for the settlement of practically all outstanding cases
                    and amplifies and clarifies the convention of April 27, 1932. The Embassy
                    understands that Mr. King and Mr. Turkel will make detailed analysis of and
                    reports on the enclosed draft convention.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Ambassador:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Edwin C.
                            Wilson</hi></signed><lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Counselor of
                        Embassy</hi></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>[For text of convention and protocol between the United States and France
                        respecting double taxation, signed July 25, 1939, see Department of State
                        Treaty Series No. 988, or 59 Stat. 893.]</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
